    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a lineup heavily incentivized by stolen base upside and favorable platoon matchups against middle-tier pitching. The standout play of the day is **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (79.13), whose elite sprint speed and favorable platoon advantage against Luis Castillo drive his top-tier rating. While **Trea Turner** (74.58) and **Kyle Schwarber** (73.46) also offer high-floor production, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** benefits from the highest situational boost (+14% platoon adjustment), making him our premier catalyst for both R and SB categories. **Junior Caminero** (70.15) remains a high-upside anchor against Brandon Young, with a solid +12% SP skill boost compensating for park factors.\n\nStrategic rotation decisions hinge on efficiency scores, as we aim to maximize our 5x5 footprint. **Caleb Durbin** (68.37) is a strong start against Mitch Bratt, buoyed by a +19% SP skill advantage that makes him a more efficient play than **Carson Benge** (58.64). The most difficult bench decision involved **Lars Nootbaar** (50.13), whose marginal score narrowly kept him in the lineup over bench depth; his rating suffered due to a significant -11% platoon penalty against Alec Gamboa. We are currently holding **Yordan Alvarez**, **Christian Yelich**, **Kazuma Okamoto**, and **Gary Sanchez** on the bench due to team off-days, while players like **Nelson Velazquez**, **Casey Schmitt**, **Josh Smith**, **Hector Rodriguez**, **Jacob Gonzalez**, **Luis Pena**, and **Pedro Ramirez** remain available for emergency spot-starts if the \"Lineup Pending\" status results in late scratches for **Dillon Dingler** or **Chase Meidroth**. Note that all current scores are projections; should the wind patterns shift or a pitcher be swapped, we will reassess the viability of **Dillon Dingler** (46.17) and **Chase Meidroth** (45.68), both of whom are hovering near our bench-threshold floor.",
